<p>The templates are made in Pixelmator Pro, but a phone app can't open a <code>.pxd</code>. So each template also exists as a <b>layer pack</b>: the same design taken apart into numbered PNGs that line up exactly when you stack them. Words are never baked in — you type them yourself in Geist — and backdrops are flat, with no grid lines or guides.</p>

<h2 id="pack">What's in a layer pack</h2>
<div class="im-table-scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>01-page-colour.png</code></td><td>The canvas colour (ink, paper or red)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>02-backdrop.png</code></td><td>A flat colour, gradient or rays, when the design has one</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>03-photo (swap for yours).png</code></td><td>The photo, already in its shape — replace it with yours</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>04-black-tint (N%).png</code></td><td>A dark wash so white words read on the photo</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>05-graphics.png</code></td><td>Chips, the red dot, lines, stickers, handwritten notes, frames</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>06-letterbox top / bottom.png</code></td><td>The cinematic bars, when the design has them</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>07-subject cut-out.png</code></td><td>A person cut out, standing in front of everything</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>text.txt</code></td><td>Every line of words: what it says, the Geist weight, size in px, colour, and where it goes</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>preview.jpg</code></td><td>What the finished design looks like</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Numbers say the order, bottom to top; not every design has every layer. YouTube thumbnails come at 1280 × 720; posts, stories, covers and banners at their own sizes (1080 × 1350, 1080 × 1920, 2000 × 1125, 2560 × 1440…). The packs live with the kits (<code>kits/layers/</code>, private) — copy a series folder to your phone with AirDrop or iCloud Drive.</p>

<h2 id="build">Build one in PicsArt</h2>
<ol>
	<li>Open <code>01-page-colour.png</code> as the project (it sets the size).</li>
	<li><b>Add Photo</b> each next layer in number order. Each one fills the canvas — stretch it corner to corner if the app shrinks it.</li>
	<li>Replace the photo layer with your own: Add Photo › your photo › place it under the graphics, then use <em>Cutout</em> or the shape's edge to match the original.</li>
	<li>Open <code>text.txt</code>. For each line: <b>Text</b>, type it, set the font (Geist Bold / Geist Mono / Caveat), size and colour, and put it at the given position.</li>
	<li>The red dot: <b>Shape › circle</b>, #f22f46, right after the headline's last letter.</li>
	<li>Compare with <code>preview.jpg</code>, then <b>Save as template</b> so the next one only needs a new photo and new words.</li>
</ol>
<p>The same steps work in Canva (upload the PNGs, stack them) and in Pixelmator / Photoshop on a computer.</p>

<h2 id="luts">LUTs</h2>
<p>Nine colour looks as <code>.cube</code> files — the same ones the thumbnails and Final Cut templates use. Apps that import a LUT (VN, CapCut, Final Cut, Premiere, Resolve, Pixelmator) take them directly; for the others use the slider recipes on <a href="/concept/looks/">Looks &amp; LUTs</a>.</p>
